I misread the question. Here is how it works in the repo.

Your machines: give yourself one notmuch-git repo, with a remote, and
clone it on each machine. I tested this with two databases standing in
for two machines and a bare repo as the remote. Machine A archived two
messages, machine B archived a third and unarchived one of A's, and
after a pull on each both held the same result. notmuch-git stores
one empty file per message and tag, so concurrent archives merge as
ordinary file adds and deletes; I saw no conflicts.

Setup on each machine:

    notmuch config set git.tag_prefix me::
    notmuch config set git.safe_fraction 1
    notmuch git clone <remote>
    git -C "$(notmuch config get git.path)" config pull.rebase false
    notmuch git checkout --force

The pull.rebase line is needed. notmuch git pull runs a plain git
pull, and once two machines have diverged, current git refuses to pull
until it is told how to reconcile. Without it the pull fails with "Need
to specify how to reconcile divergent branches". After that, the daily
loop is to tag, then run "notmuch git commit" and "notmuch git push";
the other machine runs "notmuch git pull". pull refuses while any me::
tag is uncommitted, so commit before pulling.

Use a repo of its own, on its default branch, rather than a branch in
lab.git. notmuch git reset is hard-wired to origin/master.

Collaborators: give each person their own repo. Archiving records
whether you owe a message anything, which is personal, so nobody needs
anyone else's archive state. With one repo each, everyone keeps the
same prefix and the same mail-tag.sh line, "-inbox -- tag:me::archived",
and "me::" means the person whose repo it is. Shared state such as
approved or superseded already comes from the list.

A single shared repo works against this. notmuch-git strips the prefix
when it stores a tag. So with prefixes pat:: and claude::, both
archives would land as the same tags/<hash>/<msgid>/archived file. I
infer that from the stored path; I did not test it. To share a repo,
everyone has to use one common prefix, such as lab::, and put the name
inside the tag: lab::pat-archived. Then every checkout loads every
person's archive tags into every database. Each mail-tag.sh would also
need its owner's name, which is the "me" problem again.

